README-EDITORS -- ten editors that are not vi, and which to takeDOC/README-VI covers the vi family -- seven files, three editors -- and ifyou want vi that is where to look.  This file is the rest: two Emacsen, twoSEDTs, a line editor, a binary editor and three others.Short answer: take `beav' if you edit BINARY files and `mg' if you want asmall Emacs.  For ordinary text, take vi (README-VI) -- it is better servedhere than anything on this list.THE TEN-------  file      bytes  cio  module   source  docs      what it is  --------------------------------------------------------------------  ed        85810  yes  ed       yes     7 files   GNU ed 0.2, line editor  em        79590  no   em       --      --        a screen editor  me       110408  no   me       --      --        a screen editor  emacs    144910  yes  emacs    --      1         MicroEMACS, full  umacs     45412  yes  uMacs    --      --        MicroEMACS, small  mg        79126  yes  mg       --      3 files   MicroGnuEmacs  sedt      56568  yes  sedt     --      1         SEDT 2.6, VT100  new_e     66602  no   new_e    --      --        SEDT, generic terminal  beav      85950  no   beav     yes     1         BINARY editor  e         66786  no   e        --      --        SEDT, third buildA star in DOC/INDEX means the program needs Microware's `cio'.  It shipshere, so on this disk that costs nothing; it is one more file to carry ifyou take the editor away.  DOC/README-CIO.THREE OF THESE ARE ONE EDITOR-----------------------------`sedt', `new_e' and `e' are three builds of Anders Nilsson's SEDT: `sedt'is the VT100 build, `new_e' picks vt100 or vt220 from $TERM, and `e' is athird.  All three read the same three files, which SHIP:    SYS/sedt.keys      the key bindings    SYS/sedt.help      the help text    SYS/sedt.ruler0    the ruler line`new_e' also looks for `sedt.keys_vt220', which ships too.  Take ONE of thethree and the three SYS files with it.AND TWO ARE ONE EMACS`emacs' and `umacs' are MicroEMACS large and small.  `emacs' readsUSR/LIB/EMACS -- eighteen files of macros, a `.rc' and a help text -- andthey ship.  `umacs' reads SYS/umacs.hlp.  `mg' is a different program(MicroGnuEmacs) with Emacs key bindings and no macro language.TAKE THIS IF------------  beav    you edit BINARY.  It is the only thing on this disk that edits a          file as bytes with a proper screen -- `hexed' dumps to a text          editor and back, which is a different idea -- and its source is          here.  Needs no Microware module.  mg      you want Emacs key bindings in 79K with no macro language and          three files of documentation.  emacs   you want the macros.  145K plus USR/LIB/EMACS.  umacs   you want Emacs bindings in 45K.  Its module is called `uMacs'.  sedt    you have used SEDT.  It is a DEC-style keypad editor and the key          bindings are not like anything else here.  ed      you are writing a script that edits a file without a screen.          BUT: GNU ed 0.2 makes its temporary file at `/r0', which os9exec          cannot provide, and stops at once with `module not found'.  It          will work on a machine that has an /r0.  DOC/README-RUNNING.  em, me  no documentation and no source; try them only if the above do          not suit.IF YOU CAN TAKE ONLY ONE FILEFor text: vi -- see DOC/README-VI, which recommends `elvis'.For binary: `beav', 85,950 bytes, needs no Microware module.THE HAZARD IF YOU TAKE SEVERAL`umacs' reports its module as `uMacs'.  OS-9's name comparison iscase-insensitive, so `uMacs' and a hypothetical `umacs' would collide --they do not here, because nothing else claims either.  Every other editorin this list reports its own filename.  `tools/module_census.py' listsevery name on the disk that differs from its file.